// std's stop against a real console's Ctrl-Break (spec §17, stop; internals.md, Asked to stop),
// sent by tests/helpers/console_break.py to programs in process groups of their own. A program
// that takes no Stop is ended by it, as before; one that waits learns of it, keeps it for every
// later wait, and is ended at once by the second; a task's wait is cancelled, and a later wait
// in another task still waits, until the Ctrl-Break, which it returns.
//# only: windows: GenerateConsoleCtrlEvent is Windows'; the Linux and macOS layers are compiled by Stop/every_layer
//# needs: python
//# build waiter: waiter.ctx
//# build plain: plain.ctx
//# build driver: main.ctx
//# $ {driver} {home}/tests/helpers/console_break.py {waiter} {plain}
//# timeout: 180
//# exit: 0
//# stdout:
//| plain:
//|   ready
//| plain: ended by Ctrl-Break (0xC000013A)
//| once:
//|   not asked yet
//|   ready
//|   asked: interrupt
//|   still asked: interrupt
//| once: exit code 0
//| twice:
//|   not asked yet
//|   ready
//|   asked: interrupt
//|   still asked: interrupt
//|   ready
//| twice: ended by Ctrl-Break (0xC000013A)
//| tasks:
//|   first wait: task::cancelled
//|   a timed wait: not asked
//|   second wait: still waiting
//|   ready
//|   second wait: interrupt
//| tasks: exit code 0
